<h2>Beltone and the AARP campaign feedback loop</h2>

<p>
  Beltone needed landing pages for campaigns running across AARP's website,
  magazine, and bulletin channels. The initial web pages underperformed. AARP
  provided direct feedback, and we rebuilt using it.
</p>

<p>
  The decisive change was the contact form: larger, more usable, and designed
  for an older audience rather than defaulting to the standard Marketo form
  layout. It's a useful reminder that audience-appropriate interaction design
  often beats copy optimization &mdash; particularly when the audience is
  seniors, which is directly relevant to senior living.
</p>

<h2>B2B webinar signups</h2>

<p>
  On the corporate side, webinar landing pages were producing almost nothing.
  One page published in March 2022 generated zero signups. After I developed a
  template and a documented content strategy mapping how prospects move through
  the funnel, two pages published in April and May 2022 produced
  <strong>over 80 and over 90 signups</strong> respectively.
</p>

<p>
  The underlying fix was strategic rather than cosmetic. Every page had been
  built as an isolated attempt to convert cold traffic into a sales
  conversation. Building an actual journey &mdash; with content that earned the
  next step rather than demanding it &mdash; was what changed the numbers.
</p>

<h2>Tobias Hearing Center redesign</h2>

<p>
  I led the redesign and SEO overhaul for Tobias Hearing Center. The changes
  were deliberately restrained: header, hero section, background colors, and
  some minor layout work. The practice achieved its
  <strong>peak annual call volume in October</strong> and an
  <strong>all-time record for booked appointments in December 2020</strong>.
</p>

<p>
  I include this one specifically because it argues against over-building.
  The temptation on a dated site is a full rebuild. A focused set of changes to
  the elements doing the most work produced a record year without the cost,
  risk, and migration exposure of a full redesign.
</p>
